season":2018,"team":"NO","distance":0.711,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2020,"team":"DET","distance":0.7227,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"ATL","distance":0.7246,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dan Quinn"},{"season":2020,"team":"CIN","distance":0.7987,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Jets went 3-14 in 2025 and were outscored 503 to 300. Neither side held up, as the offense ranked 29th in EPA per play and the defense 31st in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the lowest neutral-script pass rate and the lowest Cover 6 rate in the league.","offense":"The Jets offense struggled, ranking 25th or worse in all five main efficiency measures. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 3rd lowest 13 personnel usage in the league. Justin Fields ran the passing game, with Mason Taylor leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Jets had one of the worst defenses in the league, ranking 28th or worse in three of the five main efficiency measures. Will McDonald was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Jowon Briggs, who had 4 sacks while playing only 51% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the lowest Cover 6 rate in the league.","special":"The Jets were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 97% of field goals, which was the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 26% of the money on the books. The offense also turned over more of its roster than most, with 38% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Justin Fields played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 1,259 yards. Targets were spread around, with Mason Taylor and Garrett Wilson leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Breece Hall handled most of the work in the backfield with 77% of the carries. On defense, Will McDonald led the team with 8 sacks.","coaching":"Tanner Engstrand called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Steelers and the 2022 Commanders. Their defense looked most like the 2022 Lions under Aaron Glenn and the 2018 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 17th in EPA per play in 2024 to 29th in 2025, a big step back. The defense moved from 20th in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 31st in 2025, a big step back. The biggest change in approach was Cover 6 rate, which went from 5% to 2%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}